\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Continuous wearable EEG is increasingly worn alongside a second consumer device that reports
heartbeat timing. We model the cardiac field artifact (CFA) in low-channel ear-EEG as a disturbance
that is repetitive in \emph{normalized cardiac phase}, and reject it with a template learned in
phase coordinates from the EEG itself, driven only by R-peak instants and never by the ECG waveform.
Evaluated non-causally with the phase warp frozen at the mean beat, the estimator reduces exactly to
average artifact subtraction (AAS), the standard heartbeat-locked template method; we claim no
novelty for that special case and place the contribution on what it does not provide: a timing-only
interface, which allows the reference to originate from a separate wearable across which a
sample-aligned ECG waveform cannot be transported but beat instants can, and a per-beat
iterative-learning recursion, running online at $O(1)$ cost per sample, in the two modes a wearable
can actually deploy---buffered, at one beat of latency, and strict causal, at none. On behind-the-ear
EEG from a public wearable epilepsy corpus the phase-locked template stands $28.8$~dB above a
circular-shift null. Scoring all three modes held-out on identical samples, buffering retains a
median @@RETBUF@@\% of the batch suppression, whereas strict-causal operation retains none at the
@@PREDMAD@@~ms R-peak prediction error an exponential-average predictor achieves; an injected-jitter
sweep places the knee at a few milliseconds and so locates the loss in anchor prediction rather than
in the template. A held-out comparison of the phase and
fixed-lag coordinates bounds their difference at @@DABS@@~dB at the RR variability these records
exhibit.
\end{abstract}

% ===========================================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Wearable EEG is moving toward continuous, day-scale monitoring, and many users already wear a second
device that reports heartbeat timing from ECG or PPG. In ear-centered montages the cardiac field
artifact (CFA)---the volume-conducted electrical field of the heart \cite{dirlich1997}---is often
prominent, because the electrodes sit close to neck and vascular pathways. It is distinct from the
scanner-related ballistocardiogram emphasized in EEG-fMRI work \cite{niazy2005}, but it shares the
property that makes that literature's methods work: it recurs, with stable morphology, once per
heartbeat.

That property has been exploited since \emph{average artifact subtraction} (AAS) \cite{allen1998}:
epoch at detected R-peaks, average to estimate the recurring waveform, subtract. Optimal basis sets
\cite{niazy2005} generalize the fixed template to a subspace tracking epoch-to-epoch variation. Both
are offline, both index the template by elapsed time from the R-peak, and both were developed where
reference and EEG are digitized by one instrument. The alternative family---ECG-referenced adaptive
cancellation \cite{widrow1975,haykin2014,sayed2008}---is a natural baseline but needs the ECG
\emph{waveform}, sample-aligned to the EEG. Blind source separation \cite{jung2000} needs more
channels than an earable has, and learned denoisers are benchmarked mainly on high-density montages
\cite{zhang2021}.

The starting point of this paper is a deployment constraint rather than an algorithmic gap. A
companion wearable exposes a heartbeat \emph{event stream}, not a synchronized ECG trace; wrist PPG
carries no ECG waveform at all; and cross-device latency and jitter are unknown and time-varying.
Beat timing, by contrast, is one scalar per cardiac cycle and crosses the device boundary intact.
Our measurements make the trade concrete: after beat-synchronous pooling the ear-EEG artifact
retains appreciable coherence with the ECG in the lower band, so a sample-aligned waveform reference
\emph{would} carry usable information---the obstacle is obtaining one. An estimator consuming only
$\{t_k\}$ therefore admits a strictly larger set of reference devices, at no measured cost here.

We accordingly formulate CFA suppression as rejection of a disturbance that is repetitive in
\emph{normalized cardiac phase} $\phi=(t-t_k)/T_k$ rather than in elapsed time. Repetitive control
and iterative learning control (ILC) are mature tools for exactly this class of problem
\cite{hara1988,longman2000}, and phase is the coordinate in which the disturbance is genuinely
periodic when the beat period varies. Our contribution is not the averaging operation, which is
AAS; it is (i) the phase coordinate, embedded in a warp family that contains fixed-lag AAS as one
endpoint, so that ``fixed lag or normalized phase'' becomes a measured question rather than an
assumption (Sec.~\ref{sec:phase_coordinate}); (ii) the timing-only interface, which is what admits a
separate-device reference (Note~\ref{note:timing_only}); and (iii) a per-beat ILC recursion in phase
coordinates running online at $O(1)$ per sample, in a buffered one-beat-latency mode
(Sec.~\ref{sec:causal_sol}) and a strict zero-latency mode (Sec.~\ref{sec:strict_causal}), with the
price of each measured rather than asserted. We also state the method's principal cost explicitly: a template
indexed by cardiac phase cannot distinguish the artifact from a genuinely phase-locked cortical
response, and the heartbeat-evoked potential (HEP) \cite{park2019} is such a response.
A companion note \cite{hep_note} quantifies how much phase-locked energy the template removes from
the $200$--$600$~ms HEP interval and characterizes a QRS-limited variant that leaves it alone; we
carry the scope statement, not the measurement, here.

The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:problem_formulation} states the
signal model in phase coordinates and the timing-only CFA-removal problem.
Section~\ref{sec:noncausal_repetitive_rejection} gives the non-causal and causal estimators.
Section~\ref{sec:experiments} reports experiments on real ear-EEG and semi-synthetic data.
Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} summarizes the findings and their scope.


% ===========================================================================
\section{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:problem_formulation}

Following repetitive-control modeling conventions \cite{hara1988,longman2000}, we model the
measured ear-EEG signal as
\begin{equation}
y(t) = s(t) + d_r(t) + n(t), \qquad s(t) = s_a(t) + s_p(t),
\label{eq:meas}
\end{equation}
where $d_r(t)$ is the heartbeat-synchronous repetitive cardiac disturbance, $n(t)$ aggregates
asynchronous interference and sensor noise, and the neural signal splits into a cardiac-asynchronous
part $s_a(t)$ and a phase-locked part $s_p(t)$. Explicit generative models of the ECG waveform
itself \cite{sameni2007} are deliberately not required. The disturbance period is set by R-peak
timing, with beat interval
\begin{equation}
T_k = t_{k+1} - t_k,
\label{eq:Tk}
\end{equation}
where $t_k$ is the $k$-th detected R-peak instant. We index the disturbance by cardiac phase,
\begin{equation}
\phi_k(t) = \frac{t - t_k}{T_k^{\gamma}\,\bar T^{\,1-\gamma}}, \quad t\in[t_k,t_{k+1}),
\qquad
d_r(t) = m\!\left(\phi_k(t)\right),
\label{eq:phase_model}
\end{equation}
where $\bar T$ is the running mean beat interval, $\gamma\in[0,1]$ a warp exponent, and $m(\cdot)$
one-periodic, $m(\phi+1)=m(\phi)$.

Equation~\eqref{eq:phase_model} is deliberately a one-parameter family, because the two conventions
in use are its endpoints. At $\gamma=1$ it is \emph{normalized cardiac phase}: $\phi_k(t)\in[0,1)$
and the disturbance is exactly periodic however $T_k$ varies, which is what places the problem
inside repetitive control rather than beside it. At $\gamma=0$ the warp is frozen at the mean beat,
$\phi$ reduces to elapsed time in units of $\bar T$, and Eq.~\eqref{eq:phase_model} is fixed-lag
epoching---the coordinate used by AAS \cite{allen1998}, in which the cycle maps to $[0,T_k/\bar T)$
and periodicity holds only as far as $T_k\approx\bar T$. Intermediate $\gamma$ interpolates,
$\gamma=1/2$ giving a square-root warp of the kind used to rate-correct systolic intervals. Neither
endpoint is obviously right: systole is closer to time-locked to the R-peak, while diastole absorbs
most of the beat-to-beat variation. We therefore leave $\gamma$ free, and
Sec.~\ref{sec:phase_coordinate} measures it rather than assuming it.

\begin{assumption}\label{asm:cfa_dominant}
CFA at the ear is dominated by the repetitive component $d_r(t)$; the phase-asynchronous terms
$s_a(t)$, $n(t)$ are zero-mean under phase-synchronous averaging, with DC offset and slow drift
removed by preprocessing; and $T_k$ is accurately locked and slowly varying, driven mainly by
respiratory sinus arrhythmia (RSA).
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption}\label{asm:neural_not_phase_locked}
The asynchronous neural background $s_a(t)$ has, across beats, effectively uniform phase relative to
the cardiac cycle, and is therefore preserved by phase-synchronous cancellation. No such claim is
made for $s_p(t)$: a component that is a function of $\phi$ alone is, by construction,
indistinguishable from $d_r$ to any estimator that observes only $y$ and $\{t_k\}$. We therefore do
not assume $s_p\equiv 0$; we admit that the estimated template absorbs it and measure how large it
is, in a companion note \cite{hep_note}.
\end{assumption}

\begin{problem}\label{prob:cfa_removal}
Find the phase-indexed template $\mhat(\phi)$ such that subtracting it from the measured signal
recovers the asynchronous neural signal:
\begin{equation}
\hat{s}(t) = y(t) - \mhat\!\left(\phi_k(t)\right) \approx s_a(t).
\label{eq:cfa_goal}
\end{equation}
Under Assumptions~\ref{asm:cfa_dominant}--\ref{asm:neural_not_phase_locked}, $\mhat$ can be
estimated from the phase-locked structure of $y(t)$ alone.
\end{problem}

\begin{noteenv}\label{note:timing_only}
Because $m$ is a function of $\phi_k(t)$, which is determined entirely by the R-peak instants
$\{t_k\}$, Problem~\ref{prob:cfa_removal} depends only on beat \emph{timing} and never on the ECG
\emph{waveform}. Any wearable heartbeat source---smartwatch ECG, wrist PPG, or any device delivering
beat instants---is therefore sufficient. This is the property a reference-driven canceller does not
have, and the reason the formulation is worth separating from the averaging it reduces to offline.
\end{noteenv}

\begin{noteenv}\label{note:hep}
The converse of Note~\ref{note:timing_only} is that timing is also \emph{all} the estimator has.
Phase-locked cortical activity---most notably the HEP, a genuine response time-locked to the
cardiac cycle \cite{park2019}---is absorbed into $\mhat$ along with the artifact, and no learning
rule recovers it, the separation not being identifiable from $(y,\{t_k\})$. The method is therefore
positioned for settings in which heartbeat-correlated activity is nuisance contamination;
a companion note \cite{hep_note} measures the affected component and gives a restricted-support
variant that leaves the HEP interval untouched.
\end{noteenv}

\subsection{Discrete Phase Grid}
\label{sec:phase_grid}

To instantiate Problem~\ref{prob:cfa_removal} on the sample grid, let $\hat{t}_k$ be the estimated
$k$-th R-peak instant and $f_s$ the sampling rate. The cycle is divided into
$M = \operatorname{round}(f_s \bar T)$ phase bins, so that one bin is one sample wide at the mean
heart rate, and sample $n$ of beat $k$ is assigned the bin
\begin{equation}
j_k(n) \;=\; \operatorname{round}\!\left(M\,\frac{n/f_s - \hat t_k}
{T_k^{\gamma}\,\bar T^{\,1-\gamma}}\right) \bmod M ,
\label{eq:warp_family}
\end{equation}
the sampled form of Eq.~\eqref{eq:phase_model}, with the same warp exponent and the same two
endpoints.

\begin{noteenv}\label{note:support}
Because the grid spans the whole cycle, the template needs no fixed analysis window and there is no
epoch-overlap bookkeeping at high heart rates: every sample of every beat maps to exactly one bin. A
restricted
template---to protect a phase band, as in \cite{hep_note}---is imposed by setting
$\mhat(j)=0$ outside the retained bins rather than by shortening an epoch.
\end{noteenv}

% ===========================================================================
\section{Repetitive Disturbance Rejection}
\label{sec:noncausal_repetitive_rejection}

\subsection{Non-Causal Cancellation, and its Relation to AAS}
\label{sec:noncausal_sol}

Offline, the solution of Problem~\ref{prob:cfa_removal} is the phase-synchronous average. Collect
the samples that fall in bin $j$ over the $N_r$ learnable beats of the record,
$B_j = \{\,n : j_{k(n)}(n) = j\,\}$, and set
\begin{equation}
\mhat(j) \;=\; \frac{1}{|B_j|}\sum_{n \in B_j} y(n),
\qquad
\hat{s}(n) \;=\; y(n) - \mhat\!\left(j_{k(n)}(n)\right).
\label{eq:est}
\end{equation}

At $\gamma=0$, with the support restricted to a fixed post-R epoch,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:est} \emph{is} average artifact subtraction \cite{allen1998}: the same epochs, the
same average, the same subtraction. We state this rather than contrast against it, and claim no
novelty for the non-causal mode. Its standard analysis carries over unchanged---the phase-locked
term survives the average while the asynchronous terms of Eq.~\eqref{eq:meas} average down as
$1/N_r$, so template leakage falls at $\mathrm{SD}/\sqrt{N_r}$---and we do not reproduce it here.
Two things do not carry over, and they are what the rest of the paper is about: the coordinate in
which the epochs are aligned (Eq.~\eqref{eq:warp_family}, measured in
Sec.~\ref{sec:phase_coordinate}), and the fact that Eq.~\eqref{eq:est} is a batch operation, which a
streaming earable cannot perform. We note only that the $1/N_r$ decay assumes beats mutually
uncorrelated at a given phase; where part of the neural background is weakly cardiac-coupled the
floor is inflated but still decays, and Sec.~\ref{sec:real_eeg_validation} measures it directly with
a phase-scramble null rather than assuming it.

\subsection{Buffered Causal Cancellation}
\label{sec:causal_sol}

For streaming operation the batch average of Eq.~\eqref{eq:est} is replaced by an
exponentially-weighted ILC recursion in the same phase coordinate, updated once per learnable beat
at each bin $j$:
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:causal_ilc}
\begin{align}
\mhat^{(k)}(j) &= \mhat^{(k-1)}(j) + a\,\bigl[\,y_k(j) - \mhat^{(k-1)}(j)\,\bigr],
\label{eq:causal_ilc_update}\\
\hat{s}_{k-1}(n) &= y(n) - \mhat^{(k-1)}\!\left(j_{k-1}(n)\right),
\label{eq:causal_ilc_buffered}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $y_k(j)$ is the beat-$k$ sample assigned to bin $j$ and $a\in(0,1]$ is a tunable learning
gain. Equation~\eqref{eq:causal_ilc_update} is a first-order filter in the beat domain with pole
$1-a$, trading an effective averaging span of about $1/a$ beats against a steady-state leakage of
$a/(2-a)$ times the per-bin asynchronous variance; the supervisor freezes the update on premature or
missed beats.

Equation~\eqref{eq:causal_ilc_buffered} is the \emph{buffered} mode: beat $k-1$ is cancelled with
the template learned through beat $k-1$, at one beat of latency. What the one-beat wait buys is an
\emph{exact} phase index---once $t_k$ has arrived, $T_{k-1}$ is measured, so the bin assignment of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:warp_family} is the same one the non-causal estimator would have used. The only
difference from Eq.~\eqref{eq:est} is then the finite averaging span, and
Sec.~\ref{sec:streaming_modes} measures what that costs.

\subsection{Strict Causal Cancellation}
\label{sec:strict_causal}

At zero added latency neither $t_k$ nor $T_k$ has arrived when the samples of beat $k$ must be
cleaned, so both must be predicted. Writing $\hat T_k$ for the period predicted from the beats
already seen and $\hat t_k = t_{k-1} + \hat T_k$ for the predicted anchor, the canceller becomes
\begin{equation}
\hat{s}_k(n) = y(n) - \mhat^{(k-1)}\!\left(
\operatorname{round}\!\Bigl(M\,\frac{n/f_s - \hat t_k}{\hat T_k^{\gamma}\,\bar T^{\,1-\gamma}}\Bigr)
\bmod M \right).
\label{eq:strict}
\end{equation}
Two distinct errors enter, and the phase coordinate separates them. An anchor error $\hat t_k - t_k$
translates the template bodily against the beat, which the sharp QRS deflection punishes most. A
period error $\hat T_k - T_k$ instead \emph{dilates} it, an effect that grows with distance from the
anchor and with $\gamma$: at $\gamma=0$ the warp denominator is the constant $\bar T$ and the
prediction of $T_k$ does not enter Eq.~\eqref{eq:strict} at all, whereas at $\gamma=1$ it scales the
whole template. Strict-causal operation is therefore the regime in which the coordinate choice has a
cost as well as a benefit, and Sec.~\ref{sec:streaming_modes} reports both. Both streaming modes are
$O(1)$ per sample with $O(M)$ memory, and the update in Eq.~\eqref{eq:causal_ilc_update} is shared:
the modes differ only in what indexes the template at cancellation time.

\begin{noteenv}\label{note:detector}
Real-time cancellation requires an R-peak detector able to reject immature beats and invalidate
abnormal ones, so that both the template update and the cancellation can be held when the timing
reference is unreliable. We provide a functional robust detector and PI phase tracker for the
analyses below \cite{rt_eeg_proc_ekg_ilc}; detailed detector design is outside the scope of this
paper.
\end{noteenv}

% ===========================================================================
\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We implemented a robust R-peak detector and PI phase tracker \cite{rt_eeg_proc_ekg_ilc} and
demonstrate the canceller only on records where it achieves reliable phase locking; general-purpose
phase tracking on arrhythmic or low-quality ECG is deferred to future work.

\subsection{Real Ear-EEG Validation}
\label{sec:real_eeg_validation}
We use the two-channel behind-the-ear EEG configuration of SeizeIT2, a multicenter wearable epilepsy
corpus of long-duration multimodal recordings with focal-seizure annotations across a large cohort
\cite{seizeit2}. Each EEG channel is high-pass filtered at $0.5$~Hz, notch filtered at $50$~Hz and
resampled onto the ECG sample grid, and only supervisor-approved learnable beats enter the average.

\emph{Is there a template to learn?}
Since the true disturbance is unobservable on real recordings, we validate against a null that
shares the record's statistics but destroys phase locking: a circular time-shift of the whole peak
train preserves the RR-interval sequence while removing absolute phase, so the template rebuilt on
it contains leakage only. Averaging $200$ shifts gives a floor, and because the same leakage enters
the true-peak average, the ratio $\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{tmpl}}$ of main-lobe energies
($50$~ms pre to $150$~ms post) is bias-free; subtracting the floor before taking the per-sample root
and dividing by $\mathrm{std}(y)$ gives the phase-locked deflection $A/\mathrm{SD}$ in units of EEG
standard deviation \cite{hep_note}. On the showcase record (ds005873, sub-070, ses-01, run-20,
$N_r=851$ learnable beats of a $600$~s window) these read $28.80$~dB and $1.061$; across the dataset
$\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{tmpl}}$ exceeds $20$~dB in 319 runs from 36 subjects, with
$A/\mathrm{SD}\in[0.110,\,1.220]$, mean $0.557$. The artifact is real, repeatable, and comparable in
size to the EEG itself.

One further observation on the same record bears on the timing-only argument. The templates retain
appreciable coherence with the R-locked ECG beat over the lower part of the band, where the raw-beat
coherence is diluted by the neural background: the ECG waveform is \emph{not} uninformative here.
The consequence is that exploiting it is simply more demanding than exploiting timing---an
ECG-referenced canceller must be driven by the waveform itself, sample-aligned to the EEG, whereas
Eq.~\eqref{eq:est} consumes only the instants $\{t_k\}$ (Note~\ref{note:timing_only}). Both are
available on a single instrument; across the device boundary targeted here, only the timing is.

\subsection{Which Coordinate? Phase versus Fixed Lag}
\label{sec:phase_coordinate}

Equation~\eqref{eq:warp_family} makes the coordinate an empirical question, so we answer it on the
data rather than by argument. For each record we build the template on even-numbered beats and
measure the residual on the held-out odd-numbered beats, and vice versa, scoring by the variance
reduction each held-out beat window actually receives. The evaluation samples are identical for
every $\gamma$---only the coordinate in which the template was learned changes---and each beat
window is clipped at the next R-peak, so no sample is scored twice. Held-out scoring matters here:
an in-sample comparison would reward $\gamma=1$ merely for having more effective degrees of freedom
at short beats.

Fig.~\ref{fig:phase_hep}(a) reports the outcome next to the synthetic expectation. To fix what a
benefit would look like, we synthesised records whose artifact is deliberately mixed---a QRS
deflection at a fixed time offset from the R-peak, plus a T-wave component at a fixed normalized
phase---and swept the RR standard deviation from $0$ to $150$~ms. There the phase coordinate gains
$0.53$ to $2.68$~dB over fixed lag, and already $1.49$~dB at $4.7\%$ RR variability. On the SeizeIT2
recordings the measured advantage is instead @@DMED@@~dB (median over @@NKEPT@@ recordings with
$\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{tmpl}}\ge 10$~dB, of @@NREC@@ analyzed; range @@DMIN@@ to @@DMAX@@~dB) at a
comparable median $\mathrm{CV}_{RR}$ of @@CVMED@@\%. Restricting the comparison to the
$200$--$600$~ms interval, where a phase-locked late component would show up if there were one, gives
the same answer, a median of @@DHEPMED@@~dB.

Two things follow. First, on this corpus the ear CFA is time-locked to the R-peak across the whole
template rather than riding on cardiac phase in its late portion, so the coordinate is not what
limits performance here and the fixed-lag results elsewhere in this section are representative of
the phase-indexed estimator too. Second, this is a measurement at the RR variability these resting
monitoring records exhibit ($\mathrm{CV}_{RR}\le @@CVMAX@@\%$ among the resolvable ones), not a
general claim: the synthetic curve shows the coordinate does matter once a genuinely phase-locked
component is present, and Eq.~\eqref{eq:warp_family} is what lets this be checked rather than
assumed. We adopt the normalized-phase endpoint $\gamma=1$ for the formulation---it is where
Eq.~\eqref{eq:phase_model} is exactly periodic, it removes the epoch-overlap bookkeeping of
Note~\ref{note:support}, and writing the coordinate as a family renders the choice auditable---while
claiming no suppression gain we did not observe.

\subsection{Streaming Modes: What Latency Buys}
\label{sec:streaming_modes}

The three modes of Sec.~\ref{sec:noncausal_repetitive_rejection} are scored the same held-out way,
on identical samples, so they differ only in the information the canceller is allowed to use: the
non-causal estimator sees all other beats and the measured $t_k$, $T_k$; the buffered estimator sees
only beats $<k$ but still the measured $t_k$, $T_k$; the strict estimator sees beats $<k$ and must
predict both. The recursion of Eq.~\eqref{eq:causal_ilc_update} runs at $a=@@AGAIN@@$ with the first
@@WARMUP@@ beats reserved for warm-up, and $\hat T_k$ is an exponential average of past periods.

We report the comparison on the @@NMODE@@ recordings whose batch estimator removes at least
$@@NCMIN@@$~dB---below that there is no suppression to retain and the ratio is noise---and whose
R-peak prediction error is not dominated by detector failures (Note~\ref{note:detector}). There the
non-causal estimator achieves a median @@NCMED@@~dB. Buffering costs little: the median is
@@BUFMED@@~dB, retaining @@RETBUF@@\% of it, and the retained fraction rises monotonically with
artifact strength, so a wearable that can afford one beat of latency gives up essentially nothing
against the offline method. Strict-causal operation is a different matter. Driving
Eq.~\eqref{eq:strict} with an exponential-average period predictor gives a median R-peak prediction
error of @@PREDMAD@@~ms (MAD; @@PREDP90@@~ms at the 90th percentile), and at that error the mode
retains none of the suppression---a median @@STRICTMED@@~dB, i.e.\ it injects more than it
removes.

Fig.~\ref{fig:phase_hep}(b) locates that loss. Injecting a Gaussian anchor error of standard
deviation $\sigma_j$ into the buffered mode isolates timing error from the choice of predictor:
suppression is flat to $\sigma_j=2$~ms (@@JIT2@@\% retained), has lost a sixth by $5$~ms
(@@JIT5@@\%), half by $10$~ms (@@JIT10@@\%), and is gone by $20$~ms (@@JIT20@@\%). The scale is set
by the width of the QRS deflection, not by the beat interval, which is why it is so much tighter
than the RR variability itself. The measured prediction error sits well beyond that knee, so the
conclusion is specific and actionable: strict-causal cancellation is limited by the R-peak
\emph{predictor}, not by the template or the learning rule, and it needs anchor prediction accurate
to a few milliseconds---roughly an order of magnitude better than an exponential average of past RR
intervals delivers. Until such a predictor is demonstrated, the buffered mode is the one to deploy,
and one beat of latency is a fair price for @@RETBUF@@\% of the offline performance.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_real/fig_phase_hep.pdf}
\caption{(a) Held-out advantage of the normalized-phase coordinate over fixed lag,
$\Delta = \mathrm{VR}(\gamma{=}1)-\mathrm{VR}(\gamma{=}0)$ in dB, against robust RR variability, one
point per SeizeIT2 recording with a resolvable phase-locked template (marker area grows with
$\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{tmpl}}$); the orange curve is the synthetic expectation when the late
artifact component genuinely rides on cardiac phase. (b) Held-out suppression of the buffered mode
against injected anchor jitter $\sigma_j$, median over the same recordings, with the non-causal and
strict-causal operating points and the measured R-peak prediction error marked.}
\label{fig:phase_hep}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Synthetic Simulation}
\label{sec:synthetic_simulation}
Ground truth is unavailable on real recordings, so we close with a semi-synthetic check against a
reference-driven baseline, sweeping the ECG-to-artifact channel from linear to strongly nonlinear at
fixed artifact power. ICA-based removal needs more channels than an earable has, so the baseline is
a standard RLS canceller.
\input{tab_sweep_generated}
Table~\ref{tab:sweep} compares the linear ECG-referenced baseline (RLS-ANC) against the proposed
rejection under controlled predictability. RLS-ANC is coherence-gated, falling from $+7.5$ to
$+0.5$~dB as ECG-to-CFA coherence drops from $0.97$ to $0.33$, whereas the repetitive methods hold
near $+17$~dB throughout, estimating the disturbance from the EEG using beat timing alone. The
buffered mode again tracks the non-causal one to within a few tenths of a dB, reproducing on
synthetic ground truth what Sec.~\ref{sec:streaming_modes} measured held-out on real data.

% ===========================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
We have presented a control-theoretic view of cardiac-field-artifact removal in low-channel
ear-EEG: CFA is modeled as a disturbance repetitive in normalized cardiac phase and rejected by a
phase-indexed template learned from the EEG itself, anchored only to R-peak timing and never to the
ECG waveform. Written non-causally with the warp frozen at the mean beat, the estimator is average
artifact subtraction; the contribution lies in the phase coordinate that makes the disturbance
genuinely periodic under a varying beat interval, in the timing-only interface that lets the
reference originate from a separate wearable, and in the per-beat recursion that turns a batch
method into an $O(1)$-per-sample streaming one. On real ear-EEG that recursion is close to free at
one beat of latency and not yet viable at none: the buffered mode retains @@RETBUF@@\% of the batch
suppression, while the strict-causal mode is bounded by an R-peak anchor prediction that would have
to be accurate to a few milliseconds---a predictor problem, not a template one, and the sharpest
open question this framing raises. The scope of the claim is bounded by what a
timing-only estimator can separate: phase-locked cortical activity is not identifiable from the
artifact given only $(y,\{t_k\})$, so the method belongs where heartbeat-correlated activity is
nuisance contamination---continuous wearable monitoring, low-channel whole-brain modeling
\cite{momi2025}, BCI decoding of non-cardiac rhythms---and not in studies where the heartbeat-evoked
response is itself the signal. For those, the QRS-limited variant of \cite{hep_note} is the default we
would recommend whenever the downstream analysis is not known in advance.

More broadly, the construction---an event stream defining a normalized phase, a template learned in
that coordinate, a recursion that keeps it current---is not specific to the heartbeat: an inertial
reference could drive motion-artifact cancellation, and stereotyped transients such as eye blinks
admit an event-phase representation anchored at onset, peak and offset. Whether those regimes
support the same timing-only argument is an empirical question of the kind
Sec.~\ref{sec:phase_coordinate} poses here, and we would gate the work on the same diagnostic.

